creative_ireland_3eaa677e982c,creative_ireland,https://www.creativeireland.gov.ie/app/uploads/2025/11/CI_Ireland_GrantCalls_CreativeYouth_English_2026.pdf,,Creative Ireland Programme,"Across both strands, we are looking for creative and cultural projects that take place outside of the school setting and are rooted in the local community. These projects should adopt a creative approach and be shaped by the active input and participation of children and young people. This funding may be used for new projects or to expand an existing project or pilot.","Animation, Architecture, Augmented & virtual reality, Circus, Coding, Comedy, Cooking, Creative Digital Technology, Creative Writing, Cultural heritage, Dance (all types), Design (all kinds), Digital Art, Digital games, Fashion, Film, Irish Language Arts, Literature, Multi-disciplinary arts, Murals, Music (all genres), Participatory Arts, Podcasting, Storytelling, Street Art & Spectacle, Theatre, Traditional arts, Visual arts",funding scheme,Ireland,,"€800k in funding in 2026–2027 to deliver approximately eight projects at a maximum of €100,000 per project. €1.5m in funding in 2026–2028 to deliver up to six projects at a maximum of €250,000 per project.",,,en,Creative Youth Nurture Fund,"Projects delivered in Ireland by a partner based in Ireland. Projects delivered on a Shared Island basis with one partner based in Ireland and one partner based in Northern Ireland. Applicants will be expected to apply a youth-led approach, for example by applying the National Framework for Children and Young People’s People ’s Participation in Decision-making (Appendix 2), to enable them to identify, develop and engage in a creative project. It is critical that young people have as much agency as possible in the design of the project. Evaluation will be a key element of this initiative to help us identify potential areas for future development – therefore an evaluator must be in place from the beginning of the project. Successful applicants will be expected to participate in the evaluation process and share th",Creative Youth Nurture Fund,"Projects delivered in Ireland by a partner based in Ireland. Projects delivered on a Shared Island basis with one partner based in Ireland and one partner based in Northern Ireland. Applicants will be expected to apply a youth-led approach, for example by applying the National Framework for Children and Young People’s People ’s Participation in Decision-making (Appendix 2), to enable them to identify, develop and engage in a creative project. It is critical that young people have as much agency as possible in the design of the project. Evaluation will be a key element of this initiative to help us identify potential areas for future development – therefore an evaluator must be in place from the beginning of the project. Successful applicants will be expected to participate in the evaluation process and share th",,,2026-09-23 13:24:49.573158+00:00
creative_ireland_266e44f1c010,creative_ireland,https://www.creativeireland.gov.ie/app/uploads/2025/11/CI_Ireland_GrantCalls_CreativeYouth_EasyRead_2026.pdf,,Creative Ireland,We are looking for creative and cultural projects that take place outside of the school setting and are rooted in the local community.,"Animation, Architecture, Augmented/Virtual/Mixed Reality, Circus, Coding, Comedy, Cooking, Creative Digital Technology, Creative Writing, Cultural Heritage, Dance (all types), Design (all kinds), Digital Art, Digital Games, Fashion, Film, Irish Language Arts, Literature, Multidisciplinary Arts, Music (all genres), Participatory Arts, Podcasting, Street Arts and Spectacle, Theatre, Traditional Arts, Visual Arts, Storytelling",Grant Call,Ireland,,"€800k in funding is available in 2026–2027 to deliver approximately eight projects. The maximum funding is €100,000 per project.",,,en,Creative Youth Nurture Fund 2026–2028,"• We are looking for creative and cultural projects that take place outside of the school setting and are rooted in the local community. 
• Projects should have a creative approach and be shaped by active involvement of children and young people. 
• Funding can be for new projects or to expand an existing project or pilot. 
• We encourage different organisations to work together to build strong project teams to deliver high quality projects. 
• Your application must have a youth-led approach. It is very important that young people make as many decisions as possible in the design of the project. 
• The National Framework for Children and Young People’s Participation in Decision-making (see Appendix 2), will help you identify, develop and engage in a creative project.",Creative Youth Nurture Fund 2026–2028,"• We are looking for creative and cultural projects that take place outside of the school setting and are rooted in the local community. 
• Projects should have a creative approach and be shaped by active involvement of children and young people. 
• Funding can be for new projects or to expand an existing project or pilot. 
• We encourage different organisations to work together to build strong project teams to deliver high quality projects. 
• Your application must have a youth-led approach. It is very important that young people make as many decisions as possible in the design of the project. 
• The National Framework for Children and Young People’s Participation in Decision-making (see Appendix 2), will help you identify, develop and engage in a creative project.",Friday 27th February 2026,2026-02-27,2026-09-23 13:24:49.579972+00:00
cyprus_funding_programmes_culture_b33c4458f199,cyprus_funding_programmes_culture,https://www.fundingprogrammesportal.gov.cy/en/call/circulation-of-european-literary-works-en-2025/,https://ec.europa.eu/info/funding-tenders/opportunities/portal/screen/opportunities/topic-details/CREA-CULT-2026-LIT?order=DESC&pageNumber=1&pageSize=50&sortBy=startDate&isExactMatch=true&status=31094502,Creative Europe (CREA),"This action supports projects that translate, publish, distribute, and promote European works of fiction . This action complements the other initiatives or actions under Creative Europe support to the book and publishing sector such as the European Authors Day (reading promotion and discovery of European literature) and the European Union Prize for Literature (promotion of European emerging authors). Applicants are encouraged to look for synergies. In addition, as part of the exceptional support to Ukraine, applicants will be encouraged to propose activities facilitating the circulation/translation of books from and/or into Ukraine , as well as their translation into or from Ukrainian . In addition, projects aimed at providing books in the Ukrainian language to Ukrainian refugees and displaced people will be encouraged.",culture and audiovisual sectors,call,Cyprus,,The maximum grant per project for individual applicant (category 1) is of € 200 000 and for consortium (category 2) is of € 300 000. The maximum grant per project is: Small-scale : projects proposing at least 5 translations of eligible works can receive up to €100.000 . Medium-scale : projects proposing at least 11 t,,,en,Circulation of European literary works,Applicants will have to choose between the following categories of projects : category 1: individual applicant. category 2: consortium composed of a minimum of 2 eligible organisations,Circulation of European literary works,Applicants will have to choose between the following categories of projects : category 1: individual applicant. category 2: consortium composed of a minimum of 2 eligible organisations,,,2026-09-23 13:24:49.580024+00:00
cyprus_funding_programmes_culture_e643df5f8f75,cyprus_funding_programmes_culture,https://www.fundingprogrammesportal.gov.cy/en/call/circulation-of-european-literary-works-4/,https://ec.europa.eu/info/funding-tenders/opportunities/portal/screen/opportunities/topic-details/crea-cult-2024-lit?tenders=false&forthcoming=false&programmePart=&order=DESC&sortBy=startDate,Creative Europe (CREA),"This action will support projects that will translate, publish, distribute, and promote European works of fiction.",Culture,grant,Cyprus,,"Call Total Budget €5.000.000
Financing percentage by EU or other bodies / Level of Subsidy or Loan 60%
The grant parameters (maximum grant amount, funding rate, total eligible costs, etc) will be fixed in the Grant Agreement.
Project budget (maximum grant amount): small-scale: projects proposing translation of at least 5 books can receive up to €100.000.
medium-scale: projects proposing translation of at least 11 books can receive up to €200.000.
large scale: projects proposing translation of at least 21 books can receive up to €300.000.
The total grant per project cannot exceed €200.000 for single applicants and €300.000 for proposals submitted by a consortium (made of minimum 2 eligible organisations) with the above respective ceilings.",,,en,Circulation of European literary works,"Proposals should address: The following Call specific priorities : to strengthen the transnational circulation and diversity of European literary works; to encourage the translation, publication and promotion of works of fiction written in less widely spoken languages in order to increase their distribution in wider markets in Europe and beyond; to enlarge the audience for European books, especially to young people by actively encouraging them to discover the diversity of European literature; to contribute to strengthening the competitiveness of the book sector by encouraging cooperation between different actors within the book value chain- including booksellers and libraries; to promote the literary translator profession and the principles of visibility, good working conditions and fair remuneration of translator. In this respect publishers should ensure that the name of the translator(s) is in the published book, preferably on the cover, as well as the source language. NB: in order to continue to provide support to Ukraine in the field of books, projects aimed at providing books in the Ukrainian language to Ukrainian refugees and displaced people will be encouraged. (See the conditions of eligibility in Section 6 of this document. ) The following cross-cutting issues as described in section 1 Background. (i) inclusion and diversity, notably gender balance, and (ii) greening of Creative Europe.",Circulation of European literary works,"Proposals should address: The following Call specific priorities : to strengthen the transnational circulation and diversity of European literary works; to encourage the translation, publication and promotion of works of fiction written in less widely spoken languages in order to increase their distribution in wider markets in Europe and beyond; to enlarge the audience for European books, especially to young people by actively encouraging them to discover the diversity of European literature; to contribute to strengthening the competitiveness of the book sector by encouraging cooperation between different actors within the book value chain- including booksellers and libraries; to promote the literary translator profession and the principles of visibility, good working conditions and fair remuneration of translator. In this respect publishers should ensure that the name of the translator(s) is in the published book, preferably on the cover, as well as the source language. NB: in order to continue to provide support to Ukraine in the field of books, projects aimed at providing books in the Ukrainian language to Ukrainian refugees and displaced people will be encouraged. (See the conditions of eligibility in Section 6 of this document. ) The following cross-cutting issues as described in section 1 Background. (i) inclusion and diversity, notably gender balance, and (ii) greening of Creative Europe.",,,2026-09-23 13:24:49.580045+00:00
cyprus_funding_programmes_culture_7d4c26b8a3df,cyprus_funding_programmes_culture,https://www.fundingprogrammesportal.gov.cy/en/call/european-cooperation-projects-small-scale/,"https://ec.europa.eu/info/funding-tenders/opportunities/portal/screen/opportunities/topic-details/crea-cult-2022-coop-1;callCode=null;freeTextSearchKeyword=;matchWholeText=true;typeCodes=1,2;statusCodes=31094502,31094501;programmePeriod=null;programCcm2Id=null;programDivisionCode=null;focusAreaCode=null;destination=null;mission=null;geographicalZonesCode=null;programmeDivisionProspect=null;startDateLte=null;startDateGte=null;crossCuttingPriorityCode=null;cpvCode=null;performanceOfDelivery=null;sortQuery=startDate;orderBy=desc;onlyTenders=false;topicListKey=topicSearchTablePageState",Creative Europe (CREA),"This action will support projects involving a large diversity of players active in different cultural and creative sectors, to carry out a broad spectrum of activities and initiatives. The action is anchored in the policy framework of the Culture strand of the Creative Europe programme and its cross-cutting issues (inclusiveness, reduction of environmental impact and gender equality). European cooperation projects are open to all the cultural and creative sectors. Small-scale projects are particularly suitable to promote the access of grassroots organisations and support them in their creation of new partnerships and development of new activities and innovative ideas, such as festivals, art fairs, exhibitions, performances, etc.",Culture,projects,Cyprus,,"The maximum EU grant amount is of €200,000 per project.",,,en,European Cooperation projects Small Scale,"Order to be eligible, the applicants (beneficiaries and affiliated entities) must: be legal entities (public or private bodies) be established in one of the eligible countries, i.e.: Creative Europe Participating Countries: EU Member States (including overseas countries and territories (OCTs) non-EU countries: listed EEA countries and countries associated to the Creative Europe Programme or countries which are in ongoing ne",European Cooperation projects Small Scale,"Order to be eligible, the applicants (beneficiaries and affiliated entities) must: be legal entities (public or private bodies) be established in one of the eligible countries, i.e.: Creative Europe Participating Countries: EU Member States (including overseas countries and territories (OCTs) non-EU countries: listed EEA countries and countries associated to the Creative Europe Programme or countries which are in ongoing ne",,,2026-09-23 13:24:49.580064+00:00
het_nieuwe_instituut_3a4eb045d181,het_nieuwe_instituut,https://nieuweinstituut.nl/en/articles/tilting-axis-2027,https://nieuweinstituut.nl/en/articles/tilting-axis-2027,Nieuwe Instituut and Tilting Axis,"The 2027 Tilting Axis Fellowship will be supporting two fellows in a six-week programme centred on research, exchange, and network-building between the Caribbean and the Netherlands.","writing, film, sound, design, research, architecture, and other creative or interdisciplinary fields",Fellowship,Caribbean region,null,"Each fellow will receive a stipend of €2,500 to support the broader fellowship period. In addition to the stipend, fellows may access up to €1,000 in travel support connected to their research proposal. During the one week visit, all travel costs to and from the Netherlands, as well as the week-long programme in the Netherlands, will be covered separately by Nieuwe Instituut",,,en,Tilting Axis Fellowship 2027,"The fellowship is open to researchers, designers, writers, curators, or cultural producers based in the Caribbean region who are interested in developing their research practice and building connections with cultural institutions in the Netherlands. Applicants may work across different cultural fields and practices, including those engaging with archives, social and ecological questions, cultural memory, spatial practices, design, architecture, or digital culture. The fellowship is open to applicants with or without formal degrees or institutional affiliations. Equal priority will be given to those who can demonstrate creativity, critical thinking, and strong potential within their practice, regardless of educational background. There is no age limit for applicants. Applicants must be residents of and based in one of the following Caribbean countries or territories: Anguilla, Antigua & Barbuda, Aruba, The Bahamas, Barbados, Belize, Bermuda, Bonaire, British Virgin Islands, Cayman Islands, Cuba, Curaçao, Dominica, Dominican Republic, French Guiana, Grenada, Guadeloupe, Guyana, Haiti, Jamaica, Martinique, Montserrat, Providencia, Puerto Rico, Saba, San Andrés, St. Barthélemy, St. Eustatius, St. Kitts & Nevis, Saint Lucia, St. Vincent & the Grenadines, Saint Martin, Sint Maarten, Suriname, Trinidad & Tobago, Turks & Caicos Islands, US Virgin Islands.",Tilting Axis Fellowship 2027,"The fellowship is open to researchers, designers, writers, curators, or cultural producers based in the Caribbean region who are interested in developing their research practice and building connections with cultural institutions in the Netherlands. Applicants may work across different cultural fields and practices, including those engaging with archives, social and ecological questions, cultural memory, spatial practices, design, architecture, or digital culture. The fellowship is open to applicants with or without formal degrees or institutional affiliations. Equal priority will be given to those who can demonstrate creativity, critical thinking, and strong potential within their practice, regardless of educational background. There is no age limit for applicants. Applicants must be residents of and based in one of the following Caribbean countries or territories: Anguilla, Antigua & Barbuda, Aruba, The Bahamas, Barbados, Belize, Bermuda, Bonaire, British Virgin Islands, Cayman Islands, Cuba, Curaçao, Dominica, Dominican Republic, French Guiana, Grenada, Guadeloupe, Guyana, Haiti, Jamaica, Martinique, Montserrat, Providencia, Puerto Rico, Saba, San Andrés, St. Barthélemy, St. Eustatius, St. Kitts & Nevis, Saint Lucia, St. Vincent & the Grenadines, Saint Martin, Sint Maarten, Suriname, Trinidad & Tobago, Turks & Caicos Islands, US Virgin Islands.",15 October 2026,2026-10-15,2026-09-23 13:24:49.584935+00:00
het_nieuwe_instituut_702b7c1fa9ad,het_nieuwe_instituut,https://nieuweinstituut.nl/en/articles/open-call-nadd-archiefdag-2026,https://forms.office.com/pages/responsepage.aspx?id=-nOvvAYyuU2cuMU4_1TahsDTkSGWWQFJi-GrE_xuPPJUQU0zWjhTQ0ZORzREQUhWN1RTTFc1WVFLWCQlQCN0PWcu&route=shorturl,The Network Archives Design and Digital Culture (NADD),"Within the theme of Crafting the Archive, we will explore how craftsmanship, archiving, remembrance and resistance come together in new practices. This year, NADD is looking for contributions to fill some slots in the programme. Think of talks or presentations, show-and-tell, workshops or participatory sessions, performances or screenings, and roundtable conversations. We’re particularly interested in contributions that activate the archive through practice, foregrounding processes of making, sharing knowledge, and collective reflection.",design and digital culture,call for contributions,Netherlands,Amsterdam,We offer an honorarium and small materials budget. We also cover transport costs within the Netherlands but unfortunately can’t fund international travel.,,,en,Open Call: NADD Archive Day 2026,"We invite proposals from designers, makers, archivists, artists, researchers, cultural workers, educators, collectives, and students working across craft, design, and digital culture. We strive for an intergenerational conversation and audience and prioritize multivocality in deciding who to invite. Contributions are welcome in English or Dutch as sessions will be held in both languages.",Open Call: NADD Archive Day 2026,"We invite proposals from designers, makers, archivists, artists, researchers, cultural workers, educators, collectives, and students working across craft, design, and digital culture. We strive for an intergenerational conversation and audience and prioritize multivocality in deciding who to invite. Contributions are welcome in English or Dutch as sessions will be held in both languages.","8 April 2026, 23:59 CET",2026-04-08,2026-09-23 13:24:49.594813+00:00
nordic_culture_fund_a339ebc0734d,nordic_culture_fund,https://nordiskkulturfond.org/en/funding-programmes/give-ambitious-ideas-space-to-grow-with-enter,https://mypage.nordiskkulturfond.org/,Nordic Culture Fund,"ENTER is the Nordic Culture Fund’s programme for ambitious ideas which, in collaboration with others, can open up new perspectives and set new things in motion. The programme aims to offer space to experiment, learn as you go along and to test new directions, without necessarily knowing the concrete outcome.",arts and culture,funding programme,Nordic Region,,You can apply for up to 50 000 DKK. There is no requirement for co-funding.,,,en,ENTER,"Both individuals and organisations can apply to ENTER. You can come from the arts and culture sector or from other fields and sectors, as long as arts and/or culture play a central role in the initiative. Applicants and partners can be based both within and outside the Nordic Region. What matters is that the initiative as a whole has a clear and meaningful connection to the Nordic Region.",ENTER,"Both individuals and organisations can apply to ENTER. You can come from the arts and culture sector or from other fields and sectors, as long as arts and/or culture play a central role in the initiative. Applicants and partners can be based both within and outside the Nordic Region. What matters is that the initiative as a whole has a clear and meaningful connection to the Nordic Region.","Sep 14 – Oct 16, 2026",2027-09-14,2026-09-23 13:24:49.609850+00:00
nordic_culture_fund_ae44e082f513,nordic_culture_fund,https://nordiskkulturfond.org/en/funding-programmes/nemo-northern-european-mobility-opportunity,https://mypage.nordiskkulturfond.org/,Nordic Culture Fund,"Launched in 2025, the Northern European Mobility Opportunity (NEMO) is a programme for mobility and artistic exchange.",artists and arts practitioners,programme,One of the Nordic countries / The Republic of Ireland / The UK,,Apply for a grant of up to DKK 75 000,,early stages of their careers,en,NEMO – Northern European Mobility Opportunity,"To be eligible to apply for this programme you must be an individual artist or arts practitioner who is resident in: One of the Nordic countries One of the four nations of the UK The Republic of Ireland. The focus of the programme is on artists and arts practitioners who are in the emerging part of their artistic careers. If you are based in the UK or the Republic of Ireland, your application must include details of how you will develop a collaboration with a partner in the Nordic Region. Conversely, if you are based in the Nordic region, your application must include details of how you will develop a collaboration with a partner in the UK or the Republic of Ireland. All parties involved in the application must have clearly agreed on the scope of the partnership before the application is submitted. This programme is only open to applicants who did not receive support under NEMO in 2025. We want to ensure that opportunities are open to all. We particularly welcome applications that reflect the full diversity of identities and communities across all partner countries. You must meet the criteria below to be eligible to apply. You must be an emerging individual artist or arts practitioner resident in: The Nordic countries: Denmark, The Faroe Islands, Finland, Greenland, Iceland, Norway, Sweden, or The Åland Islands The Republic of Ireland The UK: England, Northern Ireland, Scotland, Wales Your collaborative partner (an artist(s), arts practitioner(s), or an organisation) must be based in the opposing region of where you are based: Artists resident in a Nordic country can only apply to work with a partner based in the UK or the Republic of Ireland, and vice versa . You must be applying for a new partnership or collaboration, and you must have approached the person or organisation that you want to work with. You must provide a letter or an email of agreement/support to prove this. You must have a background and track record of working as an artist or arts practitioner and can provide a CV which shows this. You can only be involved in one application to this fund. You must have a bank account in your country of residence. You must complete your collaboration within one year of receiving a grant.",NEMO – Northern European Mobility Opportunity,"To be eligible to apply for this programme you must be an individual artist or arts practitioner who is resident in: One of the Nordic countries One of the four nations of the UK The Republic of Ireland. The focus of the programme is on artists and arts practitioners who are in the emerging part of their artistic careers. If you are based in the UK or the Republic of Ireland, your application must include details of how you will develop a collaboration with a partner in the Nordic Region. Conversely, if you are based in the Nordic region, your application must include details of how you will develop a collaboration with a partner in the UK or the Republic of Ireland. All parties involved in the application must have clearly agreed on the scope of the partnership before the application is submitted. This programme is only open to applicants who did not receive support under NEMO in 2025. We want to ensure that opportunities are open to all. We particularly welcome applications that reflect the full diversity of identities and communities across all partner countries. You must meet the criteria below to be eligible to apply. You must be an emerging individual artist or arts practitioner resident in: The Nordic countries: Denmark, The Faroe Islands, Finland, Greenland, Iceland, Norway, Sweden, or The Åland Islands The Republic of Ireland The UK: England, Northern Ireland, Scotland, Wales Your collaborative partner (an artist(s), arts practitioner(s), or an organisation) must be based in the opposing region of where you are based: Artists resident in a Nordic country can only apply to work with a partner based in the UK or the Republic of Ireland, and vice versa . You must be applying for a new partnership or collaboration, and you must have approached the person or organisation that you want to work with. You must provide a letter or an email of agreement/support to prove this. You must have a background and track record of working as an artist or arts practitioner and can provide a CV which shows this. You can only be involved in one application to this fund. You must have a bank account in your country of residence. You must complete your collaboration within one year of receiving a grant.","Aug 17 – Sep 18, 2026 , 11:59 pm (CEST)",2027-08-17,2026-09-23 13:24:49.624799+00:00
photographers_gallery_london_a2f05a0e9710,photographers_gallery_london,https://thephotographersgallery.org.uk/whats-on/open-call-future-archives-whose-archive-it-anyway,https://forms.cloud.microsoft/e/6Ug8HGGT3m,The Photographers' Gallery,"What makes an archive and who decides? Traditionally stories of culture and society have been held and told by institutional collections. But what about the everyday material which is hidden, forgotten or displaced outside of these traditional records? Who tells these stories and decides who they belong to, how they are looked after and what their value is? Who are they for? Whose Archive is it Anyway? is the first in a three-part series of events exploring the future of archives and archiving. This one-day event on Friday 16 April 2027 will kickstart the conversation with talks addressing: what it means to make an archive today who decides what an archive consists of who creates an archive who an archive is for who has access",archives and archiving; photography,open call for speakers,,London,Selected speakers will receive a fee of £200.,,,en,Future of Archives: Whose Archive is it Anyway?,"This event is for anyone interested in or already involved in creating, holding, exploring, and taking care of archives, especially those not working in more traditional institutions or archives. This open call for speakers is now live. We welcome submissions of real-life experiences, rather than academic papers, which challenge or answer these or other points related to the future of archives. This call for contributors is open to all* especially those with experience of working with archives and the stories within them. *You must be 18 years or older to speak at this event.",Future of Archives: Whose Archive is it Anyway?,"This event is for anyone interested in or already involved in creating, holding, exploring, and taking care of archives, especially those not working in more traditional institutions or archives. This open call for speakers is now live. We welcome submissions of real-life experiences, rather than academic papers, which challenge or answer these or other points related to the future of archives. This call for contributors is open to all* especially those with experience of working with archives and the stories within them. *You must be 18 years or older to speak at this event.",1 November 2026,2026-11-01,2026-09-23 13:24:49.629248+00:00
villa_arson_4be7664be228,villa_arson,https://villa-arson.fr/actualites/2026/07/appel-a-candidatures-prix-marguerite-methode-keskar-2026/,https://villa-arson-assets.ams3.digitaloceanspaces.com/production/app/uploads/2026/07/29160517/Reglement-Prix-2026_-version-candidats.docx,Villa Arson,"La Fondation Marguerite et Méthode Keskar, abritée par la Fondation de France, récompense chaque année le travail d’un·e jeune artiste peintre ou sculpteur·rice de moins de 40 ans. Cette année, elle s’associe de nouveau à la Villa Arson pour attribuer ce prix à un·e alumni.",peinture ou sculpture,prix,,Villa Arson,d’un montant de 4 000 euros,,jeune artiste,fr,Prix Marguerite & Méthode Keskar 2026,Le / la candidat·e doit : avoir moins de 40 ans au cours de l’année 2026 ; être diplômé·e de la Villa Arson ; développer une pratique artistique dans le champ de la peinture ou de la sculpture.,Marguerite & Méthode Keskar Prize 2026,The candidate must: be under 40 years of age during the year 2026; be a graduate of Villa Arson; develop an artistic practice in the field of painting or sculpture.,dimanche 11 octobre 2026 à 23h59,2026-10-11,2026-09-23 13:24:50.845994+00:00
villa_arson_b6ed4d23bced,villa_arson,https://villa-arson.fr/actualites/2026/07/appel-a-candidatures-residence-de-recherche-curatoriale/,https://villa-arson-assets.ams3.digitaloceanspaces.com/production/app/uploads/2026/07/23104618/Appel-a-residence-recherche-curatoriale-2.pdf,Villa Arson,"Dans le cadre du développement de son projet artistique, pédagogique, scientifique et territorial, la Villa Arson crée une résidence curatoriale et d’expérimentation consacrée aux pratiques collaboratives et aux nouvelles médiations dans le champ de l’art contemporain.",art contemporain,résidence,France,Nice,les frais de déplacement étant pris en charge dans la limite de l’enveloppe prévue par la résidence.,,,fr,Résidence de recherche curatoriale,"La résidence est ouverte :
aux commissaires d’exposition ;
aux curateur·rices indépendant·es ou émergent·es ;
aux chercheur·euses travaillant dans le champ de l’art contemporain ;
aux professionnel·les de la culture développant une pratique curatoriale de recherche.
Les candidatures internationales sont vivement encouragées.
La résidence implique néanmoins une présence régulière à la Villa Arson tout au long de l’année universitaire.
Cette inscription dans la durée constitue une condition essentielle du projet, qui repose sur une immersion dans la vie de l’établissement, le développement de collaborations avec les étudiant·es et les partenaires locaux, ainsi que sur un travail de recherche ancré dans le territoire.
Les candidat·es sont invités à tenir compte de cette exigence, les frais de déplacement étant pris en charge dans la limite de l’enveloppe prévue par la résidence.
La résidence implique plusieurs périodes de présence réparties sur l’année universitaire.
Une attention particulière sera portée aux projets démontrant :
une réflexion critique sur les pratiques curatoriales contemporaines ;
un intérêt pour les démarches collaboratives et situées ;
une capacité à travailler avec des partenaires variés ;
une articulation forte entre recherche, transmission et expérimentation.",Curatorial Research Residency,"The residency is open to:
exhibition curators;
independent or emerging curators;
researchers working in the field of contemporary art;
cultural professionals developing a research-based curatorial practice.
International applications are strongly encouraged.
However, the residency requires regular presence at Villa Arson throughout the academic year.
This long-term commitment is an essential condition of the project, which is based on immersion in the life of the institution, the development of collaborations with students and local partners, as well as research work rooted in the territory.
Applicants are asked to take this requirement into account, with travel expenses covered within the limit of the budget allocated by the residency.
The residency involves several periods of حضور?",15 septembre 2026,2026-09-15,2026-09-23 13:24:53.378579+00:00
